# /etc/flowstarter/mcp-staging.env  —  mode 600, root-owned, written by hand.
#
# The template library (MCP server) slot on the Hetzner sites host. Copy this
# file, fill it in, and drive the container with
# /opt/flowstarter/staging/mcp-stack.sh. `docker restart` does NOT re-read an
# env file: use `mcp-stack.sh recreate` to apply a change here.
#
# This file is an EXAMPLE and is in git. Nothing here is a real value, and
# sync-supabase.sh deliberately never installs it — only the compose file
# beside it.

# ── The one secret ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every MCP tool call carries this token and the server compares it in
# constant time (apps/flowstarter-library/mcp-server/src/server.ts,
# `verifyToolAuth`). It is the only thing standing between /mcp and
# `scaffold_template`, which returns the complete sources of a template.
#
# It must be BYTE-IDENTICAL to FLOWSTARTER_MCP_INTERNAL_TOKEN in
# /etc/flowstarter/staging.env. The client refuses to construct itself with
# fewer than 32 characters (packages/agentic-codegen/src/flowstarter/
# template-library-mcp.ts), and the server refuses to boot with neither this
# nor Clerk configured, so a mismatch is not a silent failure — but it is an
# expensive one: every preview run dies at the first tool call.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
FLOWSTARTER_MCP_INTERNAL_TOKEN=replace-with-at-least-32-random-characters

# ── What must NOT be here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# DISABLE_AUTH=true. It is the local-development bypass, and it does not
# weaken the check — it removes it: `verifyToolAuth` returns an authenticated
# context for every caller before it looks at the token at all. On this box
# that would publish the template sources to anything that can reach
# 127.0.0.1:3001, which includes every container with host networking.
# `mcp-stack.sh check` fails if it finds it.
#
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY / CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY. The server accepts either Clerk
# session tokens or the internal token; the only client on this box is
# flowstarter-main, which uses the internal token. Configuring Clerk here
# would add a second way in for no caller that exists.
#
# SUPABASE_URL / SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY. They belong to the project tools
# (`get_project`, `get_user_projects`, `clone_template`,
# `scaffold_to_convex`), which nothing in the Flowstarter pipeline calls — it
# uses `search_templates`, `list_templates`, `get_template_details` and
# `scaffold_template`, none of which touch a database. Those tools throw a
# clear "Missing Supabase configuration" rather than misbehaving, which is the
# honest answer for a surface this deployment does not use. Adding a
# service-role key would hand the box's database to a service that has no
# reason to hold one.

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# HTTP_HOST and HTTP_PORT are set in the compose file, not here: the listener
# is inside the container's own network namespace and is reached only through
# the compose file's `127.0.0.1:3001:3001` publish rule. Setting them here
# would move the one decision that matters out of the file `check` reads.
#
# MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS is set (to empty) in the compose file. There is no
# browser caller.

# ── And on the app's side, in /etc/flowstarter/staging.env ──────────────────
#
#   FLOWSTARTER_MCP_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3001/mcp
#   FLOWSTARTER_MCP_INTERNAL_TOKEN=<the same value as above>
#
# The URL points at the MCP protocol path. `/health` lives at the server's
# ROOT, and the app's probe knows that: `generation-availability.ts` REPLACES
# the pathname rather than appending to it, so `.../mcp/health` is never
# requested. The client allows plain http only on loopback; anything else must
# be https.
